SnapRAID Daemon Windows Signature verification
==============================================

The SnapRAID Daemon Windows installer is digitally signed by the author. 
Verification is NOT a requirement for installation, but it is available 
if you want to manually confirm the integrity and origin of the software
before running it.


OPTION 1: INSTALL THE ROOT CERTIFICATE
--------------------------------------

By installing the 'am_ca.crt' file, Windows will be able to automatically
verify the digital signature of this and all future SnapRAID Daemon
installers.

Steps:
1. Download 'am_ca.crt' from https://www.snapraid.it
2. Right-click 'am_ca.crt' and select "Install Certificate".
3. Select "Local Machine" and click Next (requires Admin).
4. Select "Place all certificates in the following store".
5. Click "Browse" and choose "Trusted Root Certification Authorities".
6. Click OK, then Next, then Finish.
7. Accept the security confirmation.

Once installed, right-click the installer .exe, select Properties, and
the "Digital Signatures" tab will show "This digital signature is OK."

By signing the installer with a self-signed certificate and having the
user install that certificate into their Trusted Root Certification
Authorities container, several "Unknown Publisher" warnings are
suppressed. Specifically:

    User Account Control (UAC): The elevation prompt will change from
    a high-risk Yellow banner (Unknown Publisher) to a standard Blue
    banner, correctly identifying the author.

    Security Warnings: It prevents Windows from displaying the "The
    publisher could not be verified" dialog box when the file is
    executed.

Installing a Root Certificate involves a high level of trust in the author.
By adding 'am_rootCA.crt' to your Trusted Root Store, you are granting
the author the ability to sign any software or certificate that your
computer will then trust implicitly.

- Recommended for: Home Labs, Personal Servers, and Testing environments.
- NOT Recommended for: Mission-critical production machines or
  enterprise environments where strict security policies are required.


OPTION 2: MANUAL SHA-256 HASH VERIFICATION
------------------------------------------

If you prefer not to install the certificate, you can manually verify
that the file has not been tampered with by checking its hash against
the official record.

Steps:
1. Obtain the official SHA-256 hash from https://www.snapraid.it
2. Open PowerShell on your computer.
3. Type the following command: Get-FileHash .\snapraid-daemon-*.exe
4. Compare the output to the official hash.

If the hashes match exactly, the file is authentic and safe to run, even
if Windows displays an "Unknown Publisher" warning.
